        <p><span style="font-weight: 400;">PTE essay topics are not random. Pearson draws from a well-defined pool of subject categories that has remained remarkably consistent over the years and in 2026, those patterns continue. Knowing which categories repeat most, which topics have appeared multiple times, and how to apply one reliable template across all of them is the single biggest preparation advantage you can have going into the Write Essay task.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">This article gives you all three: the 50 most repeated PTE essay topics in 2026 organised by category, a scoring-aligned 4-paragraph template, and five complete model answers you can study and adapt. Whether you are targeting 79+ for the first time or retaking after a writing score that fell short, this is where to start.</span></p>
<h2 id="what-is-the-pte-write-essay-task"><b>What Is the PTE Write Essay Task?</b></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">The Write Essay task appears once in <a href="https://www.languageacademy.com.au/pte-online-coaching">PTE Academic</a>. You are given a prompt stating an opinion, argument, or situation and asked to respond in an academic essay format. Here is what you need to know:</span></p>
<ul>
<li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1"><span style="font-weight: 400;">You have 20 minutes to plan and write your essay</span></li>
<li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1"><span style="font-weight: 400;">The required length is 200–300 words (this is the scoring sweet spot going under 200 or over 380 incurs a penalty)</span></li>
<li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1"><span style="font-weight: 400;">Your essay must be written in complete sentences and organised into paragraphs bullet points will score zero on Form</span></li>
<li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1"><span style="font-weight: 400;">The task contributes to your Writing communicative skills score</span></li>
</ul>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">There are two main question types you will encounter: discuss both views (and give your opinion), and agree/disagree (to what extent). A small number of prompts ask for causes and solutions. Your template should work across all three and the one in this article does.</span></p>
<h2 id="what-pearson-actually-scores-the-7-traits"><b>What Pearson Actually Scores &#8211; The 7 Traits</b></h2>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Most students focus on vocabulary and grammar. Those matter, but they are only two of seven scored traits. Understanding all seven is what separates students who write well from students who score well.</span></p>
