<tr>
<td><b>PROMPT</b></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">Universities should accept equal numbers of male and female students in every subject. To what extent do you agree or disagree?</span></i></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">The question of whether universities should impose gender quotas across all disciplines has become increasingly prominent in policy debates. While I understand the intention behind this proposal, I largely disagree with the idea of enforcing strict numerical equality in university admissions.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Proponents argue that gender balance in all fields helps dismantle longstanding stereotypes and encourages women to enter male-dominated sectors such as engineering and computer science. Equal representation, they contend, leads to more diverse perspectives and ultimately better outcomes for society as a whole.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">However, enforcing equal enrolment figures disregards individual academic merit and personal choice — two principles central to academic integrity. Students should be admitted on the basis of qualifications, aptitude, and genuine interest rather than gender. Imposing quotas risks admitting underprepared candidates while excluding more qualified ones, thereby undermining institutional standards. Furthermore, the disparity in subject choices often reflects genuine differences in interest rather than systemic exclusion, and conflating the two does students a disservice.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">In conclusion, rather than mandating equal enrolment, universities should focus on removing structural barriers and encouraging broader participation through targeted outreach programmes, scholarships, and mentoring. True equality lies in equal opportunity, not equal outcomes.</span></p>
<p><b>Model Answer 2 &#8211; Technology (Topic 10)</b></p>
<table>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><b>PROMPT</b></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">Robots and computers will eventually replace workers in all fields. Do you agree or disagree?</span></i></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">The rapid advancement of artificial intelligence and robotics has sparked widespread debate about the future of human employment. While machines are undeniably transforming the workforce, I disagree with the view that they will fully replace human workers across all fields.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Automation has already displaced workers in manufacturing, data processing, and transportation, and this trend will certainly continue. Tasks that are repetitive, formulaic, or physically hazardous are particularly vulnerable to replacement, and companies driven by efficiency will inevitably adopt automation wherever it is economically viable.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">However, there are domains where human qualities remain irreplaceable. Fields such as healthcare, education, creative arts, and counselling require empathy, ethical judgement, and genuine human connection — capacities that no machine has yet demonstrated reliably. Moreover, history shows that technological revolutions typically generate new categories of employment even as they eliminate others. The rise of the internet, for instance, displaced certain roles while creating entirely new industries worth trillions of dollars globally.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">In conclusion, while automation will continue to reshape the labour market, it is unlikely to eliminate the need for human workers entirely. Societies must invest in reskilling and education to ensure that workers are equipped for the roles that emerging industries will demand.</span></p>
<p><b>Model Answer 3 &#8211; Environment (Topic 26)</b></p>
<table>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><b>PROMPT</b></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">Environmental problems such as pollution and climate change affect every country. Some people think these should be handled by governments; others believe it is the responsibility of individuals. Discuss both views and give your opinion.</span></i></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Climate change and pollution rank among the most pressing challenges of our era, and the question of who bears primary responsibility for addressing them remains deeply contested. While both governments and individuals have meaningful roles to play, I believe that governments must take the lead.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Those who emphasise individual responsibility argue that collective personal choices — reducing consumption, recycling, and adopting sustainable lifestyles — can generate meaningful change. If millions of people altered their behaviour simultaneously, the cumulative impact on carbon emissions and waste production would be substantial.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">However, expecting individuals to resolve systemic environmental problems without structural support is both unrealistic and unfair. Governments hold the unique authority to implement binding legislation, regulate industrial emissions, fund renewable energy research, and enforce international agreements such as the Paris Accord. Individual action, however widespread, cannot replace coordinated policy at the national and international level. Industrial emissions, for example, dwarf household contributions to pollution and can only be curtailed through regulation.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">In conclusion, while individuals should certainly adopt more sustainable habits, the primary responsibility for tackling environmental challenges must rest with governments. Without legislation, investment, and international cooperation, individual efforts will remain insufficient in scale.</span></p>
<p><b>Model Answer 4 &#8211; Media &amp; Advertising (Topic 33)</b></p>
<table>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><b>PROMPT</b></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">Advertising is an unavoidable part of modern life. Some people say it is a positive force; others say it is overwhelmingly negative. Discuss both views and include your own opinion.</span></i></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Advertising permeates virtually every aspect of modern life, from the billboards lining city streets to the personalised content that follows users across digital platforms. While some view this as an acceptable and economically necessary phenomenon, others argue that its influence has become overwhelmingly harmful.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Supporters of advertising argue that it fuels economic growth by connecting producers with consumers, sustaining employment across creative and commercial industries, and funding media content that audiences access at no direct cost. Effective advertising also performs a legitimate informational role, helping consumers navigate an increasingly complex marketplace.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">On the other hand, critics point to advertising&#8217;s manipulative dimensions. Many campaigns deliberately exploit psychological vulnerabilities — particularly in children and young people — fostering materialism, body image anxiety, and compulsive consumption. The targeting of minors, in particular, raises serious ethical concerns that the industry has been slow to address. Furthermore, the saturation of advertising in digital environments has eroded personal privacy through the systematic and largely opaque collection of user data.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">In conclusion, while advertising serves a legitimate economic function, its negative consequences — especially for vulnerable groups — cannot be dismissed. Stronger regulatory frameworks, rather than outright prohibition, offer the most balanced path forward.</span></p>
<p><b>Model Answer 5 &#8211; Health &amp; Lifestyle (Topic 45)</b></p>
<table>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><b>PROMPT</b></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">Stress is now a major problem in many countries around the world. What are some of the factors in modern society that cause this stress, and how can we reduce it?</span></i></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Stress has emerged as one of the defining public health challenges of the modern era, affecting people across all demographics, professions, and income levels. Understanding its root causes is the essential first step towards addressing it effectively.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">The modern workplace is one of the most significant contributors to chronic stress. Long working hours, job insecurity, digital connectivity that blurs the boundary between professional and personal life, and intense performance expectations have all intensified the psychological burden on workers. Beyond employment, financial strain, social media-driven comparison, and the erosion of traditional community structures leave many individuals without adequate emotional support systems.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Addressing these causes requires coordinated action at both individual and societal levels. Employers have a responsibility to cultivate healthy workplace cultures, offer flexible working arrangements, and provide access to mental health resources. Governments can contribute by implementing workplace wellbeing regulations, funding accessible mental health services, and running public awareness campaigns that reduce the stigma surrounding psychological distress. At an individual level, regular physical exercise, mindfulness practices, and deliberate digital detoxing have all demonstrated measurable benefits in reducing stress.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">In conclusion, while stress is a complex and multifaceted issue, it is not inevitable. Through coordinated efforts by individuals, employers, and policymakers, societies can build environments that are genuinely more conducive to psychological wellbeing.</span></p>
