<h2 id="how-to-use-this-list-before-your-exam"><b>How to Use This List Before Your Exam</b></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">A list of 50 topics is only as useful as the practice strategy around it. Here is what Language Academy recommends:</span></p>
<ol>
<li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1"><b>One topic per day, timed: </b><span style="font-weight: 400;">Pick one topic, set a 20-minute timer, and write a complete 220–270 word essay. Do not look at the model answer first.</span></li>
<li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1"><b>Score it against the 7 traits: </b><span style="font-weight: 400;">After writing, check your essay against the scoring rubric: Does it address the prompt fully? Is it 200–300 words? Does it have a clear intro, two body paragraphs, and a conclusion? Did you vary your connective language?</span></li>
<li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1"><b>Prioritise high-frequency topics first: </b><span style="font-weight: 400;">Focus your first week on the ★ topics in this list particularly Education (Topics 1, 2, 4, 6) and Technology (Topics 10, 11, 13). These account for a disproportionate share of exam appearances.</span></li>
<li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1"><b>Practise with AI scoring, not just self-assessment: </b><span style="font-weight: 400;">Self-marking has limits. Language Academy&#8217;s student portal gives you a full Content, Form, Development/Structure, Linguistic Range, Grammar, Vocabulary, and Spelling breakdown for every essay you submit — the same framework Pearson uses.</span></li>
</ol>
<h2 id="conclusion"><b>Conclusion</b></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">PTE essay topics are predictable. The categories are consistent, the high-frequency topics repeat, and the scoring rubric is fixed. That means your preparation can be targeted, not scattered. The 50 topics in this guide organised by category, ranked by frequency, and paired with model answers and a reusable template give you everything you need to walk into the Write Essay task with a clear structure and a prepared mind.</span></p>
<p>The difference between a 65 and a 79 in <a href="https://www.languageacademy.com.au/pte-writing-test-practice-material">PTE Writing</a> is rarely vocabulary or grammar. It is almost always structure, addressing the prompt correctly, and staying within the 200–300 word form requirement. Master those three things, and the score will follow.</p>
